Here's a quick self-contained test you can run with `node zip-test.js` or paste into a browser console:

```javascript
const zipRegex = /^\d{5}(?:-\d{4})?$/;

const shouldMatch = ["12345", "12345-6789", "00000"];
const shouldNotMatch = ["1234", "123456", "12345-", "12345-678", "123456789", "12345 6789", "abcde", ""];

for (const zip of shouldMatch) {
  console.assert(zipRegex.test(zip), `Expected match: "${zip}"`);
}

for (const zip of shouldNotMatch) {
  console.assert(!zipRegex.test(zip), `Expected no match: "${zip}"`);
}

console.log("Done. Any failures are printed above as assertion errors.");
```

`console.assert` stays silent when a check passes and prints the message when it fails, so a clean run only shows the "Done" line.

If you're using a test framework like Jest or Vitest, here's the same thing:

```javascript
const zipRegex = /^\d{5}(?:-\d{4})?$/;

describe("ZIP code regex", () => {
  test.each(["12345", "12345-6789"])("matches %s", (zip) => {
    expect(zipRegex.test(zip)).toBe(true);
  });

  test.each(["1234", "123456", "12345-", "12345-678", "123456789", "12345 6789", "abcde", ""])(
    "rejects %p",
    (zip) => {
      expect(zipRegex.test(zip)).toBe(false);
    }
  );
});
```

Two JavaScript-specific notes:

- In JavaScript, `\d` matches only ASCII `0-9`, even with the `u` flag, so the Unicode-digit concern from earlier doesn't apply here.
- Don't add the `g` flag to a regex you reuse with `.test()`. With `g`, the regex keeps track of `lastIndex` between calls, so repeated tests can fail unexpectedly.

