```typescript
function add(a: number, b: number): number {
  return a + b;
}
```

The original JavaScript also concatenated strings (`add("a", "b")` → `"ab"`), and that quirk is exactly the kind of thing these types exist to stop. If you actually relied on it, use overloads so each call keeps a precise return type:

```typescript
function add(a: number, b: number): number;
function add(a: string, b: string): string;
function add(a: any, b: any): any {
  return a + b;
}
```

For plain arithmetic, the first version is the right call.

The function needs an `export` first, since Jest can't test what the module keeps to itself:

```typescript
// add.ts
export function add(a: number, b: number): number {
  return a + b;
}
```

```typescript
// add.test.ts
import { add } from "./add";

describe("add", () => {
  it("adds two positive numbers", () => {
    expect(add(2, 3)).toBe(5);
  });

  it("handles negative numbers", () => {
    expect(add(-2, -3)).toBe(-5);
    expect(add(-2, 3)).toBe(1);
  });

  it("handles zero", () => {
    expect(add(0, 0)).toBe(0);
    expect(add(5, 0)).toBe(5);
  });

  it("adds decimals within floating-point tolerance", () => {
    expect(add(0.1, 0.2)).toBeCloseTo(0.3);
  });

  it("propagates NaN", () => {
    expect(add(NaN, 1)).toBeNaN();
  });
});
```

That `toBeCloseTo` isn't optional. `0.1 + 0.2` is `0.30000000000000004`, and a plain `toBe(0.3)` fails.

To run TypeScript tests, Jest needs a transformer. With `ts-jest`:

```bash
npm install --save-dev jest ts-jest @types/jest typescript
npx ts-jest config:init
npx jest
```

If you went with the overloaded version, add a string case too:

```typescript
it("concatenates strings", () => {
  expect(add("a", "b")).toBe("ab");
});
```

